description: GPU and MPS memory cap
alwaysApply: true
---

# Compute

Short work stays on CPU: unit tests, function-preserving checks, and toy notebooks that finish in well under a minute. Do not put those on the GPU.

Heavy training uses the GPU. A run that would hold the CPU for a long stretch — task training, multi-seed comparisons, confirmatory schedules — sets `device: cuda` on this PC and `device: mps` on the Mac. The same code path. Record the device. Never pool CUDA and MPS.

Default memory fraction is 0.20 via `apply_compute_cap`. Do not call `torch.cuda.set_per_process_memory_fraction` anywhere else. Fractions above 0.25 require `allow_high_memory` in the config. The CUDA machine may be shared. The cap stays in place during heavy training.

Do not launch a heavy run while changing code. Run the CPU smoke first. Mark a real CUDA test with `pytest.mark.gpu`. The default pytest command excludes that marker.

Runs are resumable. If a run would exceed the cap, record that and stop. Do not raise the cap to finish a curve.
